lesson.instructions:
⚙️ Functions are reusable blocks of code — write it once, run it whenever you need it.
You define a function with the function keyword, give it a name, and put code inside { }.
👉 Your mission: define a function called greet.
Inside it, use console.log() to print any greeting message.
Structure:
function greet() {
  console.log('Hello!');
}

🔀 Conditionals let your code make decisions — run this code IF something is true.
The if statement checks a condition inside ( ) and runs the block inside { } if it's true.
num is already set to 15 for you.
👉 Your mission: write an if statement that checks if num is greater than 10.
Example:
if (num > 10) {
  console.log('Big number!');
}

🔁 Loops repeat code automatically — instead of writing the same thing 100 times.
A for loop has 3 parts inside ( ): start · condition · step
  • let i = 0  → start at 0
  • i < 3      → keep going while i is less than 3
  • i++        → add 1 each time
👉 Your mission: write a for loop that runs exactly 3 times.
Example:
for (let i = 0; i < 3; i++) {
  console.log(i);
}

🔡 Strings in JavaScript have built-in methods you can call with a dot.
.toUpperCase() converts to capitals, .toLowerCase() does the opposite.
👉 Your mission: create a variable holding any string, then call .toUpperCase() or .toLowerCase() on it and log the result.
Example:
let word = 'nexus';
console.log(word.toUpperCase());

🗂️ Objects store related data as key-value pairs — like a profile card.
Keys are property names, values can be any type. Curly braces {} define the object.
👉 Your mission: create an object variable with at least two properties.
Example:
let person = {
  name: 'Alex',
  age: 25
};

💬 Template literals use backticks (`) instead of quotes and let you embed expressions with ${ }.
This makes string building much cleaner than concatenation with +.
👉 Your mission: create a variable and log a string that embeds it using ${ }.
Example:
let name = 'Alex';
console.log(`Hello, ${name}!`);

➡️ Arrow functions are a shorter way to write functions — great for callbacks.
Syntax: const myFn = (params) => { body };
For single-expression functions you can skip the braces and return keyword.
👉 Your mission: create an arrow function called add that takes two parameters and returns their sum.
Example:
const add = (a, b) => a + b;
console.log(add(2, 3));

➕ .push() adds one or more items to the end of an array.
It modifies the original array and returns the new length.
👉 Your mission: create an array, call .push() to add an item, then log the array.
Example:
let nums = [1, 2, 3];
nums.push(4);
console.log(nums);

🔄 .map() transforms every item in an array and returns a new array.
You pass it a function — it gets called once for each item.
👉 Your mission: use .map() to double every number in an array.
Example:
let nums = [1, 2, 3];
let doubled = nums.map(n => n * 2);
console.log(doubled);

lesson.sampleOutput:
let nums = [1, 2, 3];
