lesson.instructions:
🔍 .filter() returns a new array containing only the items that pass a test.
You pass it a function that returns true to keep an item, false to drop it.
👉 Your mission: use .filter() to keep only numbers greater than 2.
Example:
let nums = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5];
let big = nums.filter(n => n > 2);
console.log(big);

lesson.sampleOutput:
let nums = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5];
let big = nums.filter(n => n > 2);
console.log(big);

lesson.instructions:
🖱️ .addEventListener() listens for a user action (like a click) and runs a function when it happens.
Syntax: element.addEventListener('event', function);
👉 Your mission: call .addEventListener() on any element — at minimum a click listener.
Example:
const btn = document.querySelector('button');
btn.addEventListener('click', () => {
  console.log('clicked!');
});

❓ The ternary operator is a compact if/else on one line.
Syntax: condition ? valueIfTrue : valueIfFalse
👉 Your mission: use a ternary to assign a value based on a condition, then log it.
Example:
let age = 20;
let status = age >= 18 ? 'adult' : 'minor';
console.log(status);

✨ The spread operator (...) expands an array or object into individual items.
It's great for combining arrays, copying objects, and passing array items as arguments.
👉 Your mission: use ... to combine two arrays into one, then log it.
Example:
let a = [1, 2];
let b = [3, 4];
let combined = [...a, ...b];
console.log(combined);

